# Application guidance

This is a private Next.js/TypeScript development app. Follow inherited host rules.
Use the reviewed GptClaw lifecycle runner; Node and pnpm run inside containers.
Validate `.gptclaw/project.yaml`; it describes this one private, ephemeral-data
service. Source lives on retained project storage. No production dependencies,
secrets, database provisioning, public sharing or host package installation is
implied. Do not put credentials into source, logs or commits.

Before material changes read this project's approved specification/design/tasks;
use proportionate planning for routine fixes. Preserve unrelated Git work and
use reviewable branches/PRs. No product specification is supplied by scaffolding.
Use the runner's test command for health tests, and container pnpm build/typecheck
for the corresponding checks. Report exact commands and outcomes. Keep the
health route and `/projects/<project-id>/` prefix working. Stop preserves source;
application data is ephemeral. Do not reset/prune shared container storage.

Use `gptclawctl deps` for dependency installation and exact add/update/remove
requests. Read the dependency runbook in the selected GptClaw repository.
Stop only this app before a dependency mutation; retain unrelated source/Git work.
Review and commit package.json and pnpm-lock.yaml changes in this app's PR.
Install hooks, private registries, executable pnpm configuration, global installs
and implicit runtime downloads are unsupported. Do not bypass the policy with
build/test scripts. Keep .gptclaw-cache/ and node_modules/ out of Git.
On a partial dependency outcome, inspect deps status and recover the same
operation ID; never overwrite an editor's changes or cancel unknown jobs.

Use the exact `.gptclaw/toolchain.json` profile through `gptclawctl toolchain` and
the reviewed lifecycle. Preserve pins and source; stop the selected app before
an authorized toolchain switch. Do not run host language installers or select
arbitrary container images. Unsupported versions need a reviewed profile PR.

Template discovery uses `gptclawctl templates list` and `templates show`.
The separate `.gptclaw/template-release.json` records the original starter;
editing app source does not change that provenance. Template upgrades are manual
reviewed application changes, not an automatic catalogue operation.
